---
description: Metadata and provenance rules for PDF files exported from ODF or other tracked sources
alwaysApply: true
---

<!-- core:start -->
A tracked PDF is a **rendered artifact**, not a source document. It is produced
from a tracked source by one command, it is never edited afterwards, and its
metadata is inherited from that source rather than typed into it.

## The PDF is an export

- Every PDF in the repository must be reproducible from a tracked source with a
  single command, run from the repository. If the source is an ODF document, its
  metadata is governed by [`odf-metadata.mdc`](odf-metadata.mdc).
- Never edit a PDF in place — not with a GUI, not with `pdftk`, not with a
  metadata tool. An edit is lost at the next export, and it makes the artefact
  unreproducible.
- Re-export after every change to the source. A stale PDF is a defect, not a
  convenience: it is the file most readers will open.

## What survives the export

LibreOffice exports a subset of the ODF metadata into the PDF. Measured on
LibreOffice 26.2.6.3, with poppler `pdfinfo`:

| ODF element | PDF field | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `<dc:title>` | `Title` | and `dc:title` in the XMP packet |
| `<dc:subject>` | `Subject` | also becomes XMP `dc:description` |
| `<meta:keyword>` | `Keywords` | joined with `", "` |
| `<meta:initial-creator>` | `Author` | **the only** source of `Author` |
| `<dc:creator>` | — | never exported |
| `<dc:description>` (AIMetrics) | — | never exported |
| `<meta:user-defined>` contributor, publisher, rights | — | never exported |

So a PDF carries title, subject, keywords and a byline, and nothing else. The
byline is the field a reader actually sees, which is why
[`odf-metadata.mdc`](odf-metadata.mdc) requires the AI agent to appear in
`meta:initial-creator` and not only in `contributor`.

If a project genuinely needs rights, publisher or the `AIMetrics` block inside
the PDF, that is a **profile decision** and it needs an explicit build step that
injects them — an XMP packet, or a `pdfmark` passed through Ghostscript — with
the step recorded next to the export command. It is never a manual edit, and it
is never assumed. Without that step, the ODF remains the authoritative record.

## Checks

Run these on every PDF that is added or re-exported:

```bash
# 1. Metadata matches the source, and an Author exists at all.
pdfinfo deck.pdf | grep -E '^(Title|Subject|Keywords|Author):'

# 2. Structure is sound.
qpdf --check deck.pdf

# 3. Page count matches the source document.
pdfinfo deck.pdf | grep '^Pages:'

# 4. The text is really in there, so a broken export cannot pass on metadata alone.
pdftotext -layout deck.pdf - | head -20
```

Do **not** assert on `Producer`, `Creator`, `PDF version`, `CreationDate`,
`File size` or the font list beyond checking that the expected fonts are present.
Those are tool-owned and change on every export, so a test that pins them fails
for reasons that are not defects.

A PDF's `Author` is empty when the source has no `meta:initial-creator`; that is
a real defect in the source, not a quirk of the export, and it is fixed in the
source.

## Further points

- Name the PDF after the source, in the same directory: `name.odg` and
  `name.pdf`. A reader must be able to find the source from the rendered file.
- Do not commit a PDF whose source is untracked or missing. If the source cannot
  be determined, the PDF is the only copy of something, and that is a decision
  for a human, not a default.
- Record the export in the source's `AIMetrics` block when the export is part of
  the change; the PDF inherits the same dates and provenance as its source.
- Keep every `.pdf` out of any generated index that assumes a text diff: a PDF
  is opaque to review, so its source is what gets reviewed.
<!-- core:end -->

<!-- local:start -->
For this project (Jordanita, `Zarquan/jordanita`):

- PDFs live beside their `.odg` in `presentations/<YYYYMMDD-NN-Name>/`, with the
  same basename — see `20260610-02-ExecutionBroker` and
  `20261020-01-RFC9457-errors`.
- Export with the headless converter, from the presentation directory, so the
  PDF output name follows the source automatically:

      soffice --headless -env:UserInstallation=file:///tmp/louser \
          --convert-to pdf --outdir . <name>.odg

  The `-env:UserInstallation` override is required in a container: without it
  LibreOffice tries to write a profile into `$HOME` and fails.
- Check the directory after exporting. A headless run can leave a lock file
  `.~lock.<name>.odg#` behind, which is not tracked and must be deleted before
  the export counts as finished. It holds the user and host that ran the
  conversion, so it is also a small information leak if it is committed.
- This project has no GPL `<meta:licence>` requirement on rendered documents;
  the licence is stated in the `rights` metadata of the source and in
  [`LICENSE`](../../LICENSE), which is CC BY-SA 4.0.
- Copyright of the rendered diagrams rests with the human author named in
  `contributor`; the agent appears as the tool that produced the artifact, not
  as the rights holder.
<!-- local:end -->
